    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 171835\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1134 AM PDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dry conditions through the weekend with near normal temperatures\n\n* Chances of precipitation return early next week\n\n &&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Wednesday.\n\nMany thunderstorms produced moderate-to-heavy rainfall yesterday, \nwith several gauges measuring between 1.50 and 2.00 inches of \nrainfall in under 2 hours - particularly across southern Mohave \nCounty. CAMs are persistent in isolated convection returning to \neastern Lincoln County early this morning between 3 to 6 AM. \nThough shear remains low in this region, PWATs remain around 1.00 \ninches with over 1200 J/kg of SBCAPE. As a result, while storms \nwill likely remain sub-severe, they will have the potential to \ncreate lightning and light-to-moderate rainfall. Otherwise, a \nclosed area of low pressure will continue to traverse eastward \nacross the southern Great Basin today, which will once again \nelevate south-southwesterly winds in our forecast area. This \nclosed low will usher dry air into the region, allowing PWATs to \ndrop back below 0.50 inches. Temperatures will remain near-normal \nthrough the weekend.\n\nEarly next week, ensemble means indicate a resurgence of PWATs in \nexcess of 1.00 inches, which will coincide with the approach of a \nclosed area of low pressure pushing into the central California \nCoast from the eastern Pacific Ocean. This low will provide the \ninstability necessary to bring a return of PoPs to the region, \nwith best chances across the Mojave Desert. Mid-week, ridging \nreturns to the Desert Southwest, which will allow temperatures to \nclimb back to above-normal.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package... \nSoutherly winds with gusts of 20 to 25 knots are expected through \nthe afternoon before speeds diminish to less than 10 knots after \nsunset while remaining out of the south to southwest. Similar winds \nare expected on Friday with gusts to around 35 knots possible in the \nafternoon. VFR conditions will prevail through Friday with FEW to \nSCT clouds with bases remaining 10kft AGL or higher. \n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Gusty south to southwest \nwinds are expected across much of the region this afternoon, with \noccasional gusts of 20 to 25 knots likely in many areas. Slightly \nhigher gusts to around 30 knots are possible in the northern Owens \nValley, where up-valley southerly flow is expected. Most locations \nwill see winds diminish after sunset, although winds across the \nwestern Mojave Desert will remain elevated into the late evening \nbefore gradually decreasing. A few isolated showers and \nthunderstorms are expected, mainly through late afternoon over \nnorthern Mohave and southeastern Lincoln counties, with direct \nimpacts not expected at area terminals. FEW to SCT clouds with bases \nremaining AOA 10kft AGL are expected into the evening, with mainly \nclear skies developing thereafter. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat \nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
